research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-102-### Gauge fixing, BRST, and BV
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-103-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-104-“Gauge fixing” is a choice of a slice for an action on a specified configuration space. Define the configuration-space topology/regularity (for example, Sobolev \(H^s\) connections and a Sobolev gauge group), base/boundary conditions, the stabilizer subgroup, and the subset on which the quotient action is free. A Coulomb or Lorenz condition, its differential along a gauge orbit, and a local slice require separate definitions and well-posedness hypotheses. The Faddeev–Popov operator is that derivative; it is invertible only on a local transverse slice after zero modes are removed. A determinant is ordinary finite-dimensional algebra only in a finite regulator. In a field theory functional integral it remains a formal determinant unless a regularized measure and limiting operation have been constructed.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-105-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:106:A finite-dimensional/local Faddeev–Popov identity can be proved from the inverse function theorem/change of variables when the slice intersects an orbit transversely and with the stated multiplicity. A global selection of one representative per orbit is a separate topological issue. Singer’s theorem is not an excuse to write “no gauge fixing exists” without specifying the bundle, group, topology, configuration domain and continuity. Gribov copies in a particular Coulomb/Landau condition and the global Singer obstruction are distinct statements. The Project Euclid full-paper endpoint blocked retrieval during this review; its abstract supports a narrowly qualified formulation only, and its proof is not available as a logical supplier here.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-107-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-108-For the local BRST complex, write the chosen convention at the item level. A standard infinitesimal convention is
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-109-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-110-$$sA=D_Ac,\qquad sc=-\tfrac12[c,c],\qquad s\psi=-\rho_*(c)\psi.$$
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-111-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-112-Here \(c\) is odd of ghost number \(+1\), \(A\) is even of ghost number \(0\), and \(\rho_*\) is the derivative of the specified representation. Verify the sign of \(sc\) against the selected gauge-action convention; changing \(A^u\) may change signs. Define the differential on every finite jet by commuting it with total derivatives. The nilpotence proof must compute \(s^2c\), \(s^2A\), and \(s^2\psi\), identifying exactly where Lie-algebra Jacobi and the representation identity enter. Then extend to the graded polynomial algebra. An antighost \(\bar c\) and Nakanishi–Lautrup variable \(b\) are a nonminimal contractible pair only after defining \(s\bar c=b,\; sb=0\) and proving the homotopy statement in the same jet algebra. This algebraic cohomology is distinct from physical-state cohomology \(\ker Q_{\mathrm{BRST}}/\operatorname{im}Q_{\mathrm{BRST}}\).
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-113-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:114:For BV, choose a single grading/sign convention and define fields, ghosts, antifields, antibracket, classical master action, and the allowed local functionals. In the irreducible Yang–Mills case the minimal classical master action encodes the classical action, gauge variation term \(A^*D_Ac\), and ghost-antifield term quadratic in \(c\). Prove the master equation and that its Hamiltonian vector field squares to zero by the graded Jacobi identity. State how gauge fixing is represented (for example, a canonical transformation/gauge-fixing fermion or Lagrangian submanifold) and prove only the algebraic claim actually used. Do not equate the classical master equation with a quantum measure or the quantum master equation.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-115-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-116-The existing root BV interface uses a degree-\(+1\) generator, whereas many physics references use a degree-\(-1\) BV Laplacian/bracket convention. Translate every sign, degree and \(\hbar\) power explicitly if those items are consumed. The formal QME is coefficientwise; it does not by itself construct an integral or prove convergence.
