      "source": "lem-qftx-sphere-gauge-evaluation-fibration",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "Let $P\\to S^r$ be a smooth principal $SU(N)$ bundle with $r\\in\\{3,4\\}$, $N\\ge2$, a chosen point $m\\in S^r$, and a chosen frame $p_m$ in its fiber. Write $\\mathcal G(P):=\\mathcal G_\\infty(P)$ for the smooth gauge group defined by the finite-atlas $C^\\infty$ section topology, let $\\mathcal G_m(P):=\\{u\\in\\mathcal G_\\infty(P):u(p_m)=e\\}$, and put $\\overline{\\mathcal G}(P):=\\overline{\\mathcal G}_\\infty(P)=\\mathcal G_\\infty(P)/Z_N$, where $Z_N$ is the finite subgroup of constant central gauge sections. Evaluation $ev_m(u)=u(p_m)$ gives a locally trivial bundle\n$$\\mathcal G_m(P)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal G(P)\\xrightarrow{ev_m}SU(N),$$\nand the quotient map $\\mathcal G(P)\\to\\overline{\\mathcal G}(P)$ is a finite covering. For a local section $s:U\\to\\mathcal G(P)$, the bundle chart has the commutative form\n$$\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathcal G_m(P)\\times U&\\xrightarrow{\\Phi_s}&ev_m^{-1}(U)\\\\\\downarrow{\\operatorname{pr}_2}&&\\downarrow{ev_m}\\\\U&\\hookrightarrow&SU(N)\\end{array}$$\nwhere $\\Phi_s(v,g)=v\\,s(g)$; the square commutes because $ev_m(v)=e$ and $ev_m(s(g))=g$. The corresponding TikZ source is:\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzcd}\n\\mathcal G_m(P)\\times U \\arrow[r,\"\\Phi_s\"] \\arrow[d,\"\\operatorname{pr}_2\"'] & ev_m^{-1}(U) \\arrow[d,\"ev_m\"]\\\\\nU \\arrow[r,hook] & SU(N)\n\\end{tikzcd}\n```\nThe evaluation bundle is numerable; both maps are Serre fibrations and have the homotopy long exact sequence.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1",
        "2.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F3",
      "source": "lem-qftx-based-gauge-sphere-homotopy",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "Let $P\\to S^r$ be any smooth principal $SU(N)$ bundle, where $r=3$ or $4$ and $N\\ge2$. Fix a frame over a point $m$ and write $\\mathcal G_{\\infty,m}(P)=\\{u\\in\\mathcal G_\\infty(P):u(p_m)=e\\}$ for the based subgroup of the smooth gauge group defined by finite-atlas $C^\\infty$ derivative seminorms; for brevity put $\\mathcal G_m(P):=\\mathcal G_{\\infty,m}(P)$. For every $j\\ge1$ there is a group isomorphism\n$$\\pi_j(\\mathcal G_m(P),e)\\cong\\pi_{j+r}(SU(N),e),$$\nand there is a bijection of pointed component sets $\\pi_0(\\mathcal G_m(P),e)\\cong\\pi_r(SU(N),e)$. These identifications are independent of the clutching class of $P$; the unbased evaluation fibration may still depend on that class.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1",
        "2.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F4",
      "source": "lem-qftx-low-unitary-homotopy-inputs",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "For every $N\\ge2$, $SU(N)$ is path connected, $\\pi_1(SU(N))=\\pi_2(SU(N))=0$, and $\\pi_3(SU(N))\\cong\\mathbb Z$. In addition, $\\pi_4(SU(2))\\cong\\mathbb Z/2$ is finite and $\\pi_5(SU(2))\\ne0$. Here $SU(2)\\cong S^3$ by the map sending a unit quaternion to its defining special-unitary matrix.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1",
        "2.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F5",
      "source": "lem-qftx-pi5-special-unitary-nonzero",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "For every integer $N\\ge2$, $\\pi_5(SU(N))$ is nonzero. The claim is only nonvanishing; no full computation of these groups is asserted.",
      "uses": [
        "2.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F6",
      "source": "thm-long-exact-sequence-of-homotopy-groups-of-a-fibration",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "For a based Serre fibration $p:(E,e_0)\\to(B,b_0)$ with fiber $F=p^{-1}(b_0)$, the sequence\n$$\\cdots\\to\\pi_n(F)\\xrightarrow{i_*}\\pi_n(E)\\xrightarrow{p_*}\\pi_n(B)\\xrightarrow{\\partial_p}\\pi_{n-1}(F)\\to\\cdots\\to\\pi_1(B)\\xrightarrow{\\partial_p}\\pi_0(F)\\xrightarrow{i_*}\\pi_0(E)\\xrightarrow{p_*}\\pi_0(B)$$\nis exact wherever there is an incoming and outgoing arrow. Basepoints are $e_0,b_0$ as appropriate. Exactness means incoming image equals the inverse image of the distinguished element. Arrows are homomorphisms where both group structures are defined; the component terms are pointed sets. The last arrow is onto precisely when $p(E)$ meets every path component of $B$.\n\nThere is a right action of $\\pi_1(B,b_0)$ on $\\pi_0(F)$: $[e]\\cdot[\\gamma]$ is the endpoint component of a lift of $\\gamma$ starting at $e$, with loop products traversed left-to-right. Its orbits are precisely the fibers of $i_*:\\pi_0(F)\\to\\pi_0(E)$, and the stabilizer of $[e_0]$ is $p_*\\pi_1(E,e_0)$. Our boundary convention gives $\\partial_p[\\gamma]=[e_0]\\cdot[\\gamma]^{-1}$. These assertions require no AC.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1",
        "2.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F6",
      "source": "thm-covering-maps-inject-fundamental-groups",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "For a covering $p:(E,e_0)\\to(B,b_0)$, the induced homomorphism $p_*:\\pi_1(E,e_0)\\to\\pi_1(B,b_0)$ is injective.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1",
        "2.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F7",
      "source": "thm-path-lifting-for-covering-maps",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "Let $p:E\\to B$ be a covering, let $\\alpha:I\\to B$ be a path, and let $e_0\\in E$ satisfy $p(e_0)=\\alpha(0)$. There is a unique path $\\widetilde\\alpha:I\\to E$ with $\\widetilde\\alpha(0)=e_0$ and $p\\circ\\widetilde\\alpha=\\alpha$.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "derivations": [
    {
      "id": "step-1-1",
      "claim": "Suppose $r=3$. By [F3, F4], $\\pi_0(\\mathcal G_m(P))\\cong\\pi_3(SU(N))\\cong\\mathbb Z$. The evaluation LES [F2, F6] has the component segment\n$$\\pi_1(SU(N))\\longrightarrow\\pi_0(\\mathcal G_m(P))\\longrightarrow\\pi_0(\\mathcal G(P))\\longrightarrow\\pi_0(SU(N)).$$\nThe outer terms are one-point sets by [F4]. The induced map on components is surjective by exactness, and its fibers are the orbits of $\\pi_1(SU(N))$ on $\\pi_0(\\mathcal G_m(P))$ in the action clause of [F6]. That acting group is trivial, so each fiber is a singleton and the middle map is a bijection. Thus $\\mathcal G(P)$ has infinitely many components. Since $\\mathcal G(P)\\to\\overline{\\mathcal G}(P)$ is a covering with $N$ sheets, components downstairs correspond to orbits of the finite deck group on components upstairs: a path downstairs lifts by [F7] to a path whose endpoint differs from its starting lift by one deck transformation, and each deck orbit is therefore one quotient component. Each orbit has at most $N$ elements, so infinitely many components remain.",
      "step": "1.1",
      "inputs": [
        "F1",
        "F2",
        "F3",
        "F4",
        "F6",
        "F7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "step-2-1",
      "claim": "Suppose $r=4$. By [F3] and [F5], $\\pi_1(\\mathcal G_m(P))\\cong\\pi_5(SU(N))\\ne0$. In the evaluation LES, the map $\\pi_1(\\mathcal G_m(P))\\to\\pi_1(\\mathcal G(P))$ has preceding term $\\pi_2(SU(N))=0$ by [F4]; exactness makes it injective. The finite covering in [F2] induces an injection $\\pi_1(\\mathcal G(P))\\hookrightarrow\\pi_1(\\overline{\\mathcal G}(P))$ by [F6]. Hence $\\pi_1(\\overline{\\mathcal G}(P))$ contains a nonzero subgroup. The two cases establish the statement for every $r,N,P$.",
      "step": "2.1",
      "inputs": [
