 {
  "citations": [
    {
      "fact": "F1",
      "source": "def-locally-trivial-fiber-bundle",
      "source_section": "Definition",
      "quote": "A **locally trivial fiber bundle with fiber $F$** is a continuous map $p:E\\to B$, an open cover $(U_i)_{i\\in S}$ of $B$, and homeomorphisms\n$$\\theta_i:p^{-1}(U_i)\\longrightarrow U_i\\times F,\\qquad \\operatorname{pr}_1\\theta_i=p|_{p^{-1}(U_i)}.$$\nHere bundle charts, including those used later for principal and associated bundles, are charts in ordinary topological spaces with ordinary product and subspace topologies. A bundle whose spaces are CGWH in addition can be considered in the CGWH fibration convention; we do not silently replace an ordinary product chart by a weaker k-product-chart assumption. Products and homeomorphisms have the meanings of [[thm-product-universal-property]] and [[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]].\n\nOn $U_i\\cap U_j$, the coordinate change has the form $(b,x)\\mapsto(b,g_{ji}(b,x))$, with continuous inverse $(b,y)\\mapsto(b,g_{ij}(b,y))$. In particular each $g_{ji}(b,-)$ is a homeomorphism of $F$. The identities $g_{ii}(b,x)=x$ and $g_{ki}(b,x)=g_{kj}(b,g_{ji}(b,x))$ hold because the corresponding chart composites cancel. No topology on a homeomorphism group of $F$ is assumed.\n\nThe bundle is **numerable** when the data include a partition $(\\rho_i:B\\to[0,1])_{i\\in S}$ with locally finite cozero sets, sum one, and\n$$\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho_i)=\\overline{\\{b:\\rho_i(b)>0\\}}\\subseteq U_i.$$\nThis is the support-subordinate convention of [[def-partition-of-unity-subordinate-to-a-cover]]. Repeating a chart with multiple indices is allowed, so a partition subordinate to a refinement can be accompanied by an explicitly assigned original chart. Merely having pointwise finite sums or cozero containment is not this specified numerating data.\n\nAn empty base forces $E$ empty and allows the empty cover and partition. The fiber $F$ may be empty: then every chart domain, hence $E$, is empty even when $B$ is nonempty. When $F$ is nonempty the charts imply surjectivity by taking one point in the chart fiber for each particular base point; this assertion does not require choosing a simultaneous section. Over a point a chart identifies $E$ with $F$. No AC is assumed in the definition.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F2",
      "source": "prop-higher-homotopy-groups-are-functorial-and-based-homotopy-invariant",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "A based map $f:(X,x_0)\\to(Y,y_0)$ induces $f_*[a]=[f\\circ a]$ on all pointed component and cubical homotopy sets. For $n\\ge1$ this is a homomorphism, identities and composition are preserved, and based homotopic maps induce equal maps. Based homotopy equivalences induce isomorphisms.",
      "uses": [
        "3.1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "derivations": [
    {
      "id": "step-1-1",
      "claim": "Define $\\Phi:B\\times H\\to E$ by $\\Phi(b,h)=s(b)h$. Every $e\\in E$ lies over a unique $b=p(e)$ and the principal action is free and transitive on $p^{-1}(b)$, so there is a unique $h$ with $e=s(b)h$. Thus $\\Phi$ is bijective. In each local principal chart, $s$ is a continuous local section and the group coordinate $h$ is continuous; these charts cover $B$, so the inverse $e\\mapsto(p(e),h(e))$ is continuous. The forward map is continuous by the action. Hence $\\Phi$ is a homeomorphism.",
      "step": "1.1",
      "inputs": [
        "F1",
        "given",
        "construct"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "step-2-1",
      "claim": "Fix $b_0\\in B$. Under $\\Phi$, the inclusion $i:H\\to E$, $h\\mapsto s(b_0)h$, and the map $r:E\\to H$, $r(e)=\\operatorname{pr}_H(\\Phi^{-1}(e))$, satisfy $r\\circ i=\\operatorname{id}_H$. Thus $H$ is a retract. If $E$ is weakly contractible, it is path connected by [A1], so its continuous image $r(E)=H$ is path connected.",
      "step": "2.1",
      "inputs": [
        "A1",
        "step 1.1",
        "given"
      ]
    },
