    "ex-qftx-translation-gauge-slice-jacobian": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-qftx-gauge-slice-and-faddeev-popov-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite-dimensional regulated gauge-slice datum** consists of integers $1\\le k\\le n$; a finite-dimensional Lie group $K$ of dimension $k$, with Lie algebra $\\mathfrak k:=T_eK$ and bracket transported from left-invariant vector fields; a chosen smooth positive left-invariant density $\\mu_K$, meaning $(L_h^K)^*\\mu_K=\\mu_K$ for $L_h^K(g)=hg$; a finite-dimensional smooth manifold $X$ of dimension $n$ with a chosen smooth positive density $\\mu_X$; and a smooth right action $\\alpha:X\\times K\\to X$, written $x\\cdot g$, with $x\\cdot e=x$ and $(x\\cdot g)\\cdot h=x\\cdot(gh)$. A smooth positive density on an $m$-manifold has coordinate form $\\rho(x)|dx^1\\cdots dx^m|$ with smooth $\\rho>0$, and transforms by the absolute coordinate Jacobian. The group $K$ and its action on $X$ are separate regulator data. They are not identified with the full gauge group or the full space of connections unless a particular finite model explicitly constructs such an action and verifies that it preserves $X$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qftx-gauge-slice-and-faddeev-popov-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Choose an embedded smooth submanifold $S\\subset X$ of codimension $k$, a smooth positive density $\\mu_S$, and an open neighborhood $U\\subset X$ of a point $s\\in S$. Embedded means that near each point of $S$, a smooth chart on $X$ identifies $S$ with $\\mathbb R^{n-k}\\times\\{0\\}$. A **gauge condition** on $U$ is a smooth map $\\chi:U\\to\\mathbb R^k$ such that $U\\cap S=\\chi^{-1}(0)$ and, for every $s'\\in U\\cap S$, $d\\chi_{s'}$ is surjective with kernel $T_{s'}S$. For every $x\\in U$, the **infinitesimal orbit map** is $R_x:=d(\\alpha_x)_e:\\mathfrak k\\to T_xX$, where $\\alpha_x(g)=x\\cdot g$; call its kernel the infinitesimal stabilizer at $x$. The **Faddeev–Popov operator** at $x$ is the ordinary linear map $M_x:=d\\chi_x\\circ R_x:\\mathfrak k\\to\\mathbb R^k$. At a slice point $s'\\in U\\cap S$, call the condition **transverse** when $M_{s'}$ is an isomorphism. Choose a positive density $\\nu$ on $\\mathfrak k$ equal to $(\\mu_K)_e$, and the standard positive density $\\lambda$ on $\\mathbb R^k$. For every $x\\in U$, the finite-dimensional Faddeev–Popov Jacobian $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(x)$ is the nonnegative density ratio defined by $M_x^*\\lambda=\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(x)\\nu$; in chosen bases it is $|\\det M_x|$, and it is zero when $M_x$ is singular.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qftx-local-faddeev-popov-slice-jacobian",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and let $(X,K,\\alpha,S,\\chi,\\mu_X,\\mu_K,\\mu_S)$ be finite-dimensional regulated gauge-slice data as defined above, with $\\dim X=n$, $\\dim K=k$, $1\\le k\\le n$, and $s_0\\in S\\cap U$. Thus $K$ acts smoothly on the right, $S$ is an embedded codimension-$k$ submanifold, $\\chi:U\\to\\mathbb R^k$ is a smooth defining submersion with $\\ker d\\chi_s=T_sS$, and the Faddeev–Popov operator is $M_s=d\\chi_s\\circ R_s$. Suppose $M_{s_0}$ is an isomorphism. For $\\Psi(g,s)=s\\cdot g$, there are neighborhoods $V$ of the identity in $K$, $W$ of $s_0$ in $S$, and $O$ of $s_0$ in $X$ such that $\\Psi:V\\times W\\to O$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. Consequently, each orbit has exactly one representative in $W$ among the representatives reached using $V$.\n\nFix group coordinates $\\xi$ near the identity, slice coordinates $z$ near $s_0$, and adapted target coordinates $(y,z)=(\\chi,z)$ near $s_0$. Write the densities there as $\\mu_K=\\rho_K(\\xi)|d\\xi|$, $\\mu_S=\\rho_S(z)|dz|$, and $\\mu_X=\\rho_X(y,z)|dy\\,dz|$. The positive Jacobian $J_\\Psi$ defined by $\\Psi^*\\mu_X=J_\\Psi(\\mu_K\\otimes\\mu_S)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nJ_\\Psi(e,s_0)=\\frac{\\rho_X(0,z_0)}{\\rho_S(z_0)}\\,\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(s_0),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}$ uses the density $(\\mu_K)_e$ and the standard density on $\\mathbb R^k$. Thus, when the target and slice densities are normalized so that $\\rho_X(0,z_0)=\\rho_S(z_0)$, the ordinary finite-dimensional orbit-to-slice Jacobian is exactly $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(s_0)$. For every compactly supported Riemann-integrable function $f:O\\to\\mathbb R$, the local change-of-variables identity is\n$$\n\\int_O f\\,d\\mu_X=\\int_{V\\times W}(f\\circ\\Psi)J_\\Psi\\,d\\mu_K\\,d\\mu_S.\n$$\n\nFor the exact multiplicity statement, fix $x\\in X$ and a relatively compact open set $\\Gamma\\subset K$ such that $x\\cdot\\overline{\\Gamma}\\subset U$. Let $F_x(g)=\\chi(x\\cdot g)$. Let $r\\ge 0$ be finite, and suppose $F_x$ has exactly $r$ zeros $g_1,\\ldots,g_r$ in the support of a smooth function $\\varphi$ compactly supported in $\\Gamma$, each $M_{x\\cdot g_i}$ is invertible, and the slice points $s_i=x\\cdot g_i$ are distinct. Choose a nonnegative $\\eta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^k)$, supported in a ball $B_C(0)$ of finite radius $C>0$, with integral $1$, and set $\\eta_\\epsilon(y)=\\epsilon^{-k}\\eta(y/\\epsilon)$. Then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\int_\\Gamma \\varphi(g)\\,\\eta_\\epsilon(F_x(g))\\,\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(x\\cdot g)\\,d\\mu_K(g)=\\sum_{i=1}^r\\varphi(g_i).\n$$\n\nIn particular, if $\\varphi=1$ near the zeros, this finite-regulator expression counts the $r$ distinct transverse intersections exactly. The theorem asserts only a local chart and this finite-dimensional multiplicity formula; it gives no global gauge section, functional determinant, or continuum path-integral measure.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
