    "thm-qftx-singer-global-gauge-section-obstruction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-smooth-partitions-of-unity-exist-on-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Every open cover of a smooth manifold admits a smooth partition of unity subordinate to it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qftx-smooth-sobolev-gauge-configuration",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be a compact, connected, oriented, boundaryless smooth manifold of dimension $d\\in\\{3,4\\}$ with a smooth Riemannian metric, let $N\\ge2$, and fix a smooth right principal $SU(N)$-bundle $\\pi:P\\to M$, a smooth connection $A_0$, and a point $p_m\\in P$ over $x_m\\in M$. Use the standard unitary representation of $SU(N)$ on $\\mathbb C^N$ and the invariant positive inner product $\\langle X,Y\\rangle=-\\operatorname{tr}(XY)$ on $\\mathfrak{su}(N)$. Define the adjoint vector bundle $\\operatorname{ad}P=P\\times_{\\operatorname{Ad}}\\mathfrak{su}(N)$ and the adjoint group bundle $\\operatorname{Ad}P=P\\times_{\\operatorname{Ad}}SU(N)$ with associated-bundle relation $[pg,z]=[p,\\operatorname{Ad}_g z]$.\n\nChoose one finite smooth bundle atlas with relatively compact coordinate domains and a subordinate partition of unity. For each integer $k>d/2+1$, let $H^k(T^*M\\otimes\\operatorname{ad}P)$ and $H^{k+1}(\\operatorname{ad}P)$ be the Sobolev completions of smooth sections in the resulting finite-atlas norms. Define the affine configuration space $\\mathcal A_k:=A_0+H^k(T^*M\\otimes\\operatorname{ad}P)$ and the set $\\mathcal G_{k+1}:=H^{k+1}(\\operatorname{Ad}P)$ of Sobolev gauge sections. The next lemma proves that the pointwise product and inverse close on this set and make it a Hilbert Lie group. Its based subset is $\\mathcal G_{k+1,m}:=\\{u\\in\\mathcal G_{k+1}:u(p_m)=e\\}$. Let $Z_N=\\{zI_N:z^N=1\\}$ be the center of $SU(N)$, viewed as constant gauge sections. Indeed, if $Z=(z_{ij})$ is central and $D=\\operatorname{diag}(t_1,\\ldots,t_N)\\in SU(N)$, then $(t_j-t_i)z_{ij}=0$. For each $i\\ne j$ one can choose such a diagonal matrix with $t_i\\ne t_j$, so $Z$ is diagonal. Commuting with the determinant-one unitary rotation whose block on the $(i,j)$ coordinates is $\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos t&\\sin t\\\\-\\sin t&\\cos t\\end{pmatrix}$ for $t\\notin\\pi\\mathbb Z$ forces the $i$th and $j$th diagonal entries equal. Hence $Z=zI_N$; determinant one gives $z^N=1$, and conversely every such scalar is central. Write $\\bar{\\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ for the set of $Z_N$-cosets with quotient topology; the next lemma also verifies that the induced pointwise operation makes it a Hilbert Lie group.\n\nLet $\\mathcal A_\\infty:=A_0+\\Omega^1(M,\\operatorname{ad}P)$ and let $\\mathcal G_\\infty$ be the smooth gauge group, with the $C^\\infty$ section topology generated by all finite-atlas derivative seminorms. Define $\\bar{\\mathcal G}_\\infty:=\\mathcal G_\\infty/Z_N$. In a local section of $P$, write a connection as a matrix-valued one-form $A$ and a gauge section as a map $u$ into $SU(N)$; the right gauge action is $A^u:=u^{-1}Au+u^{-1}du$, equivalently pullback by the gauge automorphism associated with $u$. The calculus lemma proves that this formula defines a smooth action on the Sobolev spaces above. For an adjoint section $\\xi$, set $d_A\\xi:=d\\xi+[A,\\xi]$ using the degree-one Lie-algebra-valued-form bracket. In local coordinates and an orthonormal adjoint frame, define the formal adjoint on an adjoint-valued one-form $a$ by $(d_A^*a)=-g^{ij}(\\nabla_i^{\\mathrm{LC}}a_j+[A_i,a_j])$, where $\\nabla^{\\mathrm{LC}}$ differentiates the covector index. This is the $L^2$ adjoint for the Riemannian volume and stated fiber metric; its local coefficient formula defines a distribution at Sobolev regularity.\n\nFor $A\\in\\mathcal A_k$, parallel transport of its $C^1$ connection form around piecewise smooth loops based at $p_m$ defines a subgroup $\\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A)\\subseteq SU(N)$. Call $A$ irreducible when this subgroup leaves no nonzero proper complex subspace of $\\mathbb C^N$ invariant; changing the chosen frame conjugates the subgroup and leaves this condition unchanged. Write $\\mathcal A_k^*$ and $\\mathcal A_\\infty^*$ for the irreducible loci. Once the calculus lemma verifies the group action, define orbit equivalence by $A\\sim B$ exactly when $B=A^u$ for some gauge section $u$; all resulting quotients carry the quotient topology, whose open sets are those with open inverse image under the orbit projection.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the configuration data and integer $k>d/2+1$ from the first definition. The irreducible locus $\\mathcal A_k^*$ is open, and the orbit projection\n\n$$\\pi_k:\\mathcal A_k^*\\longrightarrow \\mathcal A_k^*/\\bar{\\mathcal G}_{k+1}$$\n\nis a locally trivial smooth principal Banach Lie group bundle. For every $A\\in\\mathcal A_k^*$ there is an $\\varepsilon>0$ such that the Coulomb slice\n\n$$S_{A,\\varepsilon}:=\\{A+a:a\\in\\ker d_A^*,\\ \\|a\\|_{H^k}<\\varepsilon\\}$$\n\nlies in $\\mathcal A_k^*$, meets each orbit in its saturation exactly once, and has open saturation. The map $S_{A,\\varepsilon}\\to\\pi_k(S_{A,\\varepsilon})$ is a smooth chart of the quotient, whose quotient topology is Hausdorff.\n\nFor the $C^\\infty$ connection and gauge spaces with the section topologies specified in the first definition, the irreducible projection\n\n$$\\pi_\\infty:\\mathcal A_\\infty^*\\longrightarrow\\mathcal A_\\infty^*/\\bar{\\mathcal G}_\\infty$$\n\nis a locally trivial topological principal $\\bar{\\mathcal G}_\\infty$-bundle with the actual $C^\\infty$ quotient topology. No inverse-limit assertion alone is used to obtain this smooth-space conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-qftx-irreducible-connections-weak-contractibility",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume ZF together with the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be $S^3$ or $S^4$, let $N\\ge2$, and let $P\\to M$ be any smooth principal right $SU(N)$-bundle. Fix a smooth reference connection $A_0$, a basepoint $m\\in M$, and a frame of $P_m$. Equip the affine space $\\mathcal A_\\infty=A_0+\\Omega^1(M;\\operatorname{ad}P)$ with its $C^\\infty$ section topology, and let $\\mathcal A_\\infty^*$ be the connections whose based holonomy action on $\\mathbb C^N$ has no nonzero proper invariant complex subspace. Then $\\mathcal A_\\infty^*$ is nonempty and path-connected, and for every $A\\in\\mathcal A_\\infty^*$ and every integer $j\\ge1$, $\\pi_j(\\mathcal A_\\infty^*,A)=0$. Here $\\pi_j(X,x)$ is the based homotopy group of continuous maps $u:(I^j,\\partial I^j)\\to(X,x)$ modulo homotopies that keep $\\partial I^j$ at $x$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-qftx-reduced-gauge-group-sphere-nontriviality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r\\in\\{3,4\\}$, $N\\ge2$, and $P\\to S^r$ be any smooth principal $SU(N)$ bundle. Write $\\mathcal G(P):=\\mathcal G_\\infty(P)$ for its smooth gauge group with the finite-atlas $C^\\infty$ section topology and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}(P):=\\overline{\\mathcal G}_\\infty(P)=\\mathcal G_\\infty(P)/Z_N$ for the quotient by constant central gauge sections. Then either $\\overline{\\mathcal G}(P)$ has more than one path component or at least one of its based homotopy groups $\\pi_j(\\overline{\\mathcal G}(P),e)$, $j\\ge1$, is nonzero. More precisely, for $r=3$ it has infinitely many path components, and for $r=4$ its fundamental group is nontrivial.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-qftx-section-retract-obstruction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p:E\\to B$ be a nonempty locally trivial principal topological $H$-bundle with right $H$-action, and suppose it has a continuous section. For any $b_0\\in B$, the fiber $H$ is a retract of $E$. If $E$ is weakly contractible, then $H$ is path connected and $\\pi_j(H,e)=0$ for every $j\\ge1$.\n\nThe retraction is expressed by the commutative diagram\n$$\\begin{array}{ccc}H&\\xrightarrow{i}&E\\\\\\Big\\downarrow{\\scriptstyle\\operatorname{id}_H}&&\\Big\\downarrow{\\scriptstyle r}\\\\H&=&H\\end{array}$$\nwith TikZ source\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzcd}\nH \\arrow[r,\"i\"] \\arrow[dr,\"\\operatorname{id}_H\"'] & E \\arrow[d,\"r\"]\\\\\n& H\n\\end{tikzcd}\n```",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
